                  "content": "SELECTED: candidate_05\n\nCONFIDENCE: HIGH\n\nEXPLANATION:\nThe fix commit modifies CallerFinder.getStackForCallerOf() to add back a \"skip\" parameter and\nchanges the scan loop from `for (int index = 0; index < depth; index++)` to\n`for (int index = skip; index < depth; index++)`, along with a new comment in LogContext.java\nexplaining that skipping is \"essential for correctness\" because postProcess() can be overridden,\nproducing a stack like:\n\n  UserCode::someMethod\n  LogContext::log\n  LogContext::shouldLog\n  OtherChildContext::postProcess\n  ChildContext::postProcess   << not the real caller\n  LogContext::postProcess     << current frame\n\nWithout skipping past the LogContext/subclass frames, the class-name-matching logic in\ngetStackForCallerOf can find and stop at the wrong \"caller\" frame (an overridden postProcess()\nin an intermediate subclass), producing a corrupted/incorrect synthetic stack trace.\n\ncandidate_05 is the commit that removed this exact skip mechanism. Originally (as seen in\ncandidate_01, the initial commit), getStackForCallerOf(Class target, Throwable throwable, int skip,\nint maxDepth) took an explicit skip count and looped with `for (int index = skip; index < depth; index++)`,\nwith LogContext.java calling it as `getStackForCallerOf(LogContext.class, new Throwable(), 2, stackSize.getMaxDepth())`\n(skipping 2 known frames: postProcess() and shouldLog()).\n\ncandidate_05's diff shows:\n- LogContext.java: removes the `2` skip argument entirely, calling\n  `getStackForCallerOf(LogContext.class, new Throwable(), stackSize.getMaxDepth())`\n- CallerFinder.java: removes the `skip` parameter and its validation, and replaces\n  `for (int index = skip; index < depth; index++)` with `for (int index = 0; index < depth; index++)`,\n  adding a comment claiming this change was needed because a fixed skip count was \"problematic in\n  the face of things like Proguard, which can fold method/classes and change the number of\n  intermediate stack frames.\"\n\nThis regression removed the ability to skip known intermediate frames, relying solely on\nclass-name matching to find the caller. That approach fails when postProcess() is overridden in a\nsubclass hierarchy (multiple frames match/are adjacent to LogContext-related classes), which is\nexactly the \"synthetic stack bug\" the fix commit addresses by reintroducing a skip count (this\ntime hardcoded to 1 rather than 2, as a narrower/safer fix). No other candidate touches\ngetStackForCallerOf, CallerFinder's loop logic, or the skip parameter, confirming candidate_05 as\nthe sole introduction point of this bug.\n"
                }
              }
            ]
